316 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(Not every harmful online act is TFGBV. The gender dimension becomes central when the harm is directed at someone because of gender, sexualized, rooted in gender inequality, or disproportionately experienced by women and girls.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

317 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 95 0 R/T(1.3. Historical Context)/E(1.3. Historical Context)/Pg 18 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

318 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(The history of this agenda item is best understood as three overlapping waves of international attention, each building on the last rather than replacing it.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

319 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(The first wave established the underlying architecture of women's rights within the UN system, long before "digital" was part of the vocabulary. The UN Charter \(1945\) and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights \(1948\) laid the normative groundwork for equality between men and women. The Commission on the Status of Women \(CSW\) followed quickly, established by ECOSOC resolution 11\(II\) in 1946 as the principal global intergovernmental body dedicated to gender equality \204 making it one of the oldest functional commissions in the UN system. Three World Conferences on Women followed over the next three decades: Mexico City \(1975\), Copenhagen \(1980\) and Nairobi \(1985\), each expanding the international consensus on women's rights. The Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women \(CEDAW\) was adopted in 1979 and remains the core binding human-rights treaty on this agenda, obligating states parties to take "all appropriate measures" to eliminate discrimination against women in all its forms.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

320 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 95 0 R/E(The second wave crystallized at the Fourth World Conference on Women in Beijing in September 1995, which produced the Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action. Beijing remains the most comprehensive global policy framework on gender equality ever adopted, organized around twelve critical areas of concern, including violence against women, women and the media, education and training, and power and decision-making. )/Pg 18 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

321 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 95 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[687 0 R 0]/K[16]>>
endobj

