endobj

338 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(The third wave is institutional consolidation and the shift from "access" to "safety." In 2010, the General Assembly adopted resolution 64/289, merging four previously separate UN gender bodies \204 the UN Development Fund for Women \(UNIFEM\), the Division for the Advancement of Women, the Office of the Special Adviser on Gender Issues, and the UN International Research and Training Institute for the Advancement of Women \204 into a single composite entity, UN Women, which became operational on 1 January 2011 under founding Executive Director Michelle Bachelet. This consolidation gave gender equality work a single normative voice within the UN system for the first time.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

339 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(Through the 2000s and 2010s, as ICTs became central to education, employment and political participation, the international conversation shifted from formal rights toward measurable connectivity and skills gaps \204 the "digital gender divide" as a distinct policy problem. Then, starting roughly in the mid-2010s, the same technologies that were closing \(or failing to close\) that access gap were increasingly recognized as new vectors of harm. The CEDAW Committee's General Recommendation No. 35 \(2017\), updating the founding General Recommendation No. 19 \(1992\), was the pivotal normative moment: it explicitly extended "gender-based violence against women" to technology-mediated environments, describing violence as a continuum spanning private, public and digital settings alike.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

340 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 96 0 R/E(2025 marked a hinge point that any delegate should treat as the live, current-session anchor for this agenda. It was the thirtieth anniversary of Beijing, and the Commission used its 69th session \(CSW69, 10\20521 March 2025\) to conduct a formal review of implementation. For the first time, a CSW political declaration explicitly referenced gender-based violence occurring in cyberspace, and UN Women launched the Beijing+30 Action Agenda alongside it \204 a roadmap built on national review input from 159 Member States. Later that year, UN Women's UNiTE campaign for the 16 Days of Activism against Gender-Based Violence adopted digital violence against women and girls as its dedicated focus. Read together, the historical arc runs from formal equality \(1945\2051979\), to comprehensive policy framework \(1995\), to institutional consolidation \(2010\), to recognition of technology-facilitated violence as a treaty-scope issue \(2017\), to the current moment of translating thirty years of recognition into resourced, measurable digital-era action \(2024\2052025\).)/Pg 19 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

