392 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 98 0 R/T(he normative frameworks above set out rights and principles; the following national and regional platform-accountability laws show how several States have already begun translating those principles into enforceable rules that touch directly on technology-facilitated violence against women. Delegates can cite these as existing precedents rather than asking the Council to invent regulatory concepts from nothing.)/E(he normative frameworks above set out rights and principles; the following national and regional platform-accountability laws show how several States have already begun translating those principles into enforceable rules that touch directly on technology-facilitated violence against women. Delegates can cite these as existing precedents rather than asking the Council to invent regulatory concepts from nothing.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

393 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 98 0 R/T(\200 European Union: the Digital Services Act entered into force on 16 November 2022 and became directly applicable across all EU member states from 17 February 2024, imposing risk-assessment, due-diligence and transparency obligations on large platforms, including obligations relevant to gender-based online abuse.)/E(\200 European Union: the Digital Services Act entered into force on 16 November 2022 and became directly applicable across all EU member states from 17 February 2024, imposing risk-assessment, due-diligence and transparency obligations on large platforms, including obligations relevant to gender-based online abuse.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

394 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 98 0 R/T(\200 United Kingdom: the Online Safety Act 2023 received royal assent on 26 October 2023 and is being brought into force in phases by Ofcom, with illegal-content and child-safety duties enforceable from early 2024; it creates statutory duties of care covering harassment and intimate-image abuse.)/E(\200 United Kingdom: the Online Safety Act 2023 received royal assent on 26 October 2023 and is being brought into force in phases by Ofcom, with illegal-content and child-safety duties enforceable from early 2024; it creates statutory duties of care covering harassment and intimate-image abuse.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

395 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 98 0 R/T(\200 Australia: the Online Safety Act 2021 commenced on 23 January 2022 and gives the eSafety Commissioner, in place since 2015, a statutory adult cyber-abuse complaints scheme and an image-based abuse scheme allowing victims to seek removal of non-consensual intimate images.)/E(\200 Australia: the Online Safety Act 2021 commenced on 23 January 2022 and gives the eSafety Commissioner, in place since 2015, a statutory adult cyber-abuse complaints scheme and an image-based abuse scheme allowing victims to seek removal of non-consensual intimate images.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

396 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 98 0 R/T(\200 India: the Information Technology \(Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code\) Rules, 2021, notified on 25 February 2021, require intermediaries to operate a grievance-redressal mechanism and, for large platforms, to enable traceability of the first originator of certain flagged content.)/E(\200 India: the Information Technology \(Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code\) Rules, 2021, notified on 25 February 2021, require intermediaries to operate a grievance-redressal mechanism and, for large platforms, to enable traceability of the first originator of certain flagged content.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

397 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 98 0 R/T(The variation among these frameworks is itself instructive for delegates: some rely on statutory duties of care enforced by a dedicated regulator \(UK, Australia\), others on horizontal due-diligence obligations applied across a whole trading bloc \(EU\), and others on intermediary-liability rules tied to an existing IT statute \(India\). A resolution does not need to endorse any single model, but citing more than one shows the committee that platform accountability is achievable through several different legal architectures, which strengthens the case that a proposed mechanism is realistic rather than aspirational.)/E(The variation among these frameworks is itself instructive for delegates: some rely on statutory duties of care enforced by a dedicated regulator \(UK, Australia\), others on horizontal due-diligence obligations applied across a whole trading bloc \(EU\), and others on intermediary-liability rules tied to an existing IT statute \(India\). A resolution does not need to endorse any single model, but citing more than one shows the committee that platform accountability is achievable through several different legal architectures, which strengthens the case that a proposed mechanism is realistic rather than aspirational.)/Pg 21 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

