438 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(Intersectionality. Risk and access barriers compound across age, disability, income, race and ethnicity, migration status, sexual orientation, rural location and public visibility. A rural, low-income woman with a disability does not face "the digital divide" in the same way as an urban professional woman; a uniform, one-size-fits-all policy can therefore produce very unequal real-world outcomes even when it appears neutral on its face.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

439 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(Rights-respecting proportionality. Safety measures must be balanced against privacy, due process, encryption and freedom of expression, not pursued through unrestricted surveillance or vague content bans that could criminalize legitimate speech. The Global Digital Compact explicitly frames digital safety and human-rights protection as complementary obligations, not competing ones \204 a resolution should show it understands this is a balance to be struck, not a binary choice.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

440 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(Evidence and accountability. Commitments are only as strong as the ability to verify them. Every substantive clause should be paired with disaggregated, sex-specific data, a named implementing actor, and a measurable indicator \204 echoing the Global Digital Compact's call for comparable, sex-disaggregated methodologies to track digital-inclusion and safety commitments across states. UN Women's 2025 findings that only around 57 per cent of needed gender data is currently available, and that just one in four countries can report how much they spend on gender equality, illustrate why this principle cannot be treated as a technical footnote: without comparable measurement, "accountability" clauses are unenforceable by construction, regardless of how strongly they are worded.)/Pg 23 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

441 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 100 0 R/E(Together, these six principles function less as a checklist and more as a set of cross-checks delegates can apply to any proposed clause. A safety measure that ignores proportionality risks becoming a surveillance mandate; a data clause that ignores intersectionality risks measuring the wrong population; a platform-accountability clause that ignores the continuum of violence risks treating online harm as disconnected from the offline abuse it often accompanies. )/Pg 23 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

