460 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 101 0 R/T(1.6. Contemporary Case Studies and Violations)/E(1.6. Contemporary Case Studies and Violations)/Pg 24 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

461 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 101 0 R/T(Case Study 1 \204 Women in public life)/E(Case Study 1 \204 Women in public life)/Pg 24 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

462 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 101 0 R/E(Women journalists, politicians, activists and human-rights defenders can face coordinated online abuse intended not merely to insult but to drive them out of public participation. UN Women research emphasizes that online violence can spill into offline threats and affect women\220s safety, well-being and participation.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

463 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 101 0 R/T(Case Study 2 \204 Girls and synthetic sexual content)/E(Case Study 2 \204 Girls and synthetic sexual content)/Pg 24 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

464 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 101 0 R/E(A student whose face is placed onto explicit synthetic media can face school exclusion, family pressure, reputational harm and threats even when the content is entirely fabricated. The policy lesson is that a criminal definition based only on \215real intimate material\216 can leave victims without an effective remedy.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

465 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 101 0 R/T(Case Study 3 \204 Intimate-partner digital control)/E(Case Study 3 \204 Intimate-partner digital control)/Pg 24 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

466 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 101 0 R/E(An abusive partner may demand passwords, monitor location, inspect messages, install tracking applications or use shared cloud accounts to maintain control. The case demonstrates why TFGBV cannot be treated only as a platform-moderation issue; survivor services, domestic-violence systems, police training and digital-security assistance are also necessary.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

467 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 101 0 R/T(Intersectionality)/E(Intersectionality)/Pg 24 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

468 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 101 0 R/E(Risk can be intensified by age, disability, poverty, race/ethnicity, migration status, sexuality, rural location or public visibility. A universal policy may therefore produce unequal outcomes. Resolutions should require disaggregated data and accessible reporting mechanisms without forcing survivors to disclose unnecessary personal information.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

