486 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 102 0 R/T(1.7. Challenges in the Implementation and Enforcement)/E(1.7. Challenges in the Implementation and Enforcement)/Pg 25 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

487 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 722 0 R/E(Lack of International Regulation: )/Pg 25 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

488 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(The central obstacle facing this agenda is not a shortage of normative language; CEDAW, Beijing, the Global Digital Compact and multiple CSW outcomes already articulate the relevant rights and goals in considerable detail. The obstacle is a persistent, and in some respects worsening, gap between what has been agreed and what is actually resourced, and enforced.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

489 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(Financing is the most acute of these gaps. UN Women's Gender Snapshot 2025, produced jointly with UN DESA, found an annual financing shortfall of roughly USD 420 billion for gender equality efforts in developing countries. This is not abstract: a separate 2025 UN Women study found that one in three organizations working to end violence against women had suspended o programmes due to funding cuts, and several major donors reduced or withdrew support for gender-focused UN programmes during the year,  showing how quickly formally agreed financing commitments can be reversed.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

490 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(Shrinking civic space compounds this. Drawing on over 150 government submissions to its 2024\2052025 stocktake, UN Women found that nearly one in four countries reported a backlash against women's rights actively undermining implementation, part of a broader pattern of democratic backsliding, conflict and economic insecurity. For delegates, this means a resolution's ambition can be undercut not just by lack of resources but by active political resistance in the states meant to implement it.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

491 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 102 0 R/E(Measurement is a related challenge: funding cuts to national statistical systems mean only around 57 per cent of the data needed to track gender-equality progress is currently available, so neither the Commission nor Member States can reliably verify whether commitments are being met \204 leaving accountability mechanisms largely symbolic.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

