555 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 105 0 R/T(1.9. QARMA \205 Questions a Resolution Must Answer)/E(1.9. QARMA \205 Questions a Resolution Must Answer)/Pg 28 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

556 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 729 0 R/T(How does the delegation define the gendered digital divide beyond simple access to the internet, particularly in relation to affordability, digital literacy, online safety, algorithmic discrimination, and women\220s ability to meaningfully participate in digital spaces?)/E(How does the delegation define the gendered digital divide beyond simple access to the internet, particularly in relation to affordability, digital literacy, online safety, algorithmic discrimination, and women\220s ability to meaningfully participate in digital spaces?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

557 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 730 0 R/T(Which groups of women and girls\204including those in rural areas, conflict-affected regions, low-income communities, and marginalized populations\204face the greatest barriers to digital participation, and what concrete mechanisms does the delegation propose to ensure that international policy does not leave these groups further behind?)/E(Which groups of women and girls\204including those in rural areas, conflict-affected regions, low-income communities, and marginalized populations\204face the greatest barriers to digital participation, and what concrete mechanisms does the delegation propose to ensure that international policy does not leave these groups further behind?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

558 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 731 0 R/T(Where does the delegation place responsibility when gender-based digital harm occurs: with national governments, technology companies, social-media platforms, users, or international institutions\204and how should responsibility be divided when multiple actors contribute to the harm?)/E(Where does the delegation place responsibility when gender-based digital harm occurs: with national governments, technology companies, social-media platforms, users, or international institutions\204and how should responsibility be divided when multiple actors contribute to the harm?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

559 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 732 0 R/T(How can Member States combat online gender-based violence and technology-facilitated abuse without creating vague or excessively broad regulations that could be misused to suppress legitimate political speech, journalism, activism, or women\220s rights advocacy?)/E(How can Member States combat online gender-based violence and technology-facilitated abuse without creating vague or excessively broad regulations that could be misused to suppress legitimate political speech, journalism, activism, or women\220s rights advocacy?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

560 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 733 0 R/T(What safeguards does the delegation propose to ensure that measures addressing online harassment, intimate-image abuse, doxxing, and other forms of technology-facilitated violence protect survivors\220 privacy and dignity while simultaneously guaranteeing due process and preventing arbitrary censorship?)/E(What safeguards does the delegation propose to ensure that measures addressing online harassment, intimate-image abuse, doxxing, and other forms of technology-facilitated violence protect survivors\220 privacy and dignity while simultaneously guaranteeing due process and preventing arbitrary censorship?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

561 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 734 0 R/T(Should social-media and technology companies merely be encouraged to address gender-based digital harms, or should Member States establish legally binding obligations requiring platforms to assess risks, remove unlawful content, provide transparent reporting mechanisms, and disclose how their algorithms may disproportionately affect women and girls?)/E(Should social-media and technology companies merely be encouraged to address gender-based digital harms, or should Member States establish legally binding obligations requiring platforms to assess risks, remove unlawful content, provide transparent reporting mechanisms, and disclose how their algorithms may disproportionately affect women and girls?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

562 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 735 0 R/T(If a Member State criminalizes online gender-based violence but lacks independent judicial institutions, transparent enforcement mechanisms, or adequate survivor-protection systems, how can the international community distinguish genuine protection of women from legislation that merely expands state control over digital speech?)/E(If a Member State criminalizes online gender-based violence but lacks independent judicial institutions, transparent enforcement mechanisms, or adequate survivor-protection systems, how can the international community distinguish genuine protection of women from legislation that merely expands state control over digital speech?)/Pg 28 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

